<%- include('partials/head') %>

<section class="hero hero-compact">
  <h1>What each check means</h1>
  <p class="hero-lede">
    The score is weighted, not a simple count. A missing title tag costs ten times what a missing
    favicon does, because that is roughly how much each one matters to a search engine. This page
    is generated from the same registry the engine runs, so it never drifts out of date.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>How scoring works</h2>
  <p>
    Every check returns pass, warn or fail and carries a weight from 1 to 10. A pass earns the full
    weight, a warning earns half, a failure earns none. The score is the total earned divided by the
    total available, so a page with <%= total %> checks and <%= maxWeight %> weight points available
    is scored on what actually applies to it — checks that cannot apply, like image alt text on a page
    with no images, are reported but left out of the maths.
  </p>
  <p>
    Grades: A is 90 and above, B is 80, C is 70, D is 55, anything lower is an F.
  </p>
</section>

<% groups.forEach(function (group) { %>
  <section class="group">
    <h2><%= group.name %></h2>
    <ul class="checks">
      <% group.items.forEach(function (c) { %>
        <li class="check is-reference">
          <div class="check-head">
            <h3><%= c.label %></h3>
            <span class="weight-tag">weight <%= c.weight %></span>
          </div>
          <p class="check-message"><code><%= c.id %></code></p>
        </li>
      <% }); %>
    </ul>
  </section>
<% }); %>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>What this tool does not do</h2>
  <p>
    It reads the HTML the server returns. It does not execute JavaScript, so a page that renders its
    content client-side will look emptier here than it does in a browser — which is close to how
    some crawlers see it anyway, and is itself worth knowing.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also does not report search volume or difficulty. Those numbers come from paid data providers.
    Intent classification here is rule-based and transparent: you can read the patterns in
    <code>lib/keywords.js</code> and change them.
  </p>
</section>

<%- include('partials/foot') %>
